  "problem": "For the sets $X={f,g,h}$ and $Z=X\\times X,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(f,g)\\in Z$\n- $(f,g,h)\\in Z$\n- $(h,h)\\in Z$",
  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $X\\times X$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $X.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${X}^{2}=X\\times X$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/1718bd6f3f2eaa8dd8fe581b0d938b3b5f1928d5e56a11a324f145e7a481fabe.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times X$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(f,f),(f,g),(f,h),(g,f),(g,g),(g,h),(h,f),(h,g),(h,h)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
